<InfoLayout
  eyebrow="Methodology"
  title="Deliverables first, not task chains"
  description="Why this PMO's Steps database tracks outcomes a PM can report on, not a checklist of busywork."
>
  <p>
    Most schedules decompose into tasks — smaller and smaller chores that add up to a deliverable,
    but that a sponsor never actually asks about. This PMO's Steps database inverts that: a step
    is a deliverable in its own right, something a team commits to producing, not a chore on the
    way to producing it.
  </p>

  <h2>What changes in practice</h2>
  <p>
    A step has an owner, a status, and a definition of done that means something outside the
    project team. "Draft the migration runbook" is a step. "Open a doc and start typing" is not —
    it's how the step gets done, and nobody needs to track it separately.
  </p>

  <h2>Where it shows up</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/#tab-scheduling-prep">Step Schedule</a> preps the database ahead of team 1:1s by
    carrying forward steps that applied to similar past projects — so a schedule never starts from
    a blank database. <a href="/#tab-planning">Planning Synthesis</a> writes each team's confirmed
    deliverable as its own step row once planning sessions land on what's actually being built,
    then proposes milestones from the finished schedule.
  </p>
</InfoLayout>
